#include (../Settings/defs.ves)

Before defining an integration, we need to say that what kind of functions are
``measurable to integrate''. There is a general way to define it, but we first
focus for real-valued functions.
Everywhere in this section, $(X,\mu)$ denotes a measure space.
Furthermore, after this moment, we use the fact that $\Mf_\mu$ is $\sigma$-field
(because of \Cref{thm:caratheodory}) without any mention.

#label(defin:measurable-fnt)
useenv defin [Measurable functions] {
    Let $Y$ be a topological space. We say that a function $f:X->Y$ is
    \it{$\mu$-measurable}\index{function!$\mu$-measurable} if $\{f\in V\}$
    is $\mu$-measurable for every open set $V$ in $Y$. Recall that $\{f\in V\}$
    is an abbriviation of $\{x : f(x) \in V\}$.
}

Below proposition gives an equivalent statement of $\mu$-measurable functions.

#label(prop:measurable-fnt)
useenv prop {
    Let $f:X->\R$ be a function, Then the followings are equivalent.
    useenv enumerate [label=(\roman*)] {
        \item $f$ is $\mu$-measurable.
        \item $\{f \in F\}$ is $\mu$-measurable for every closed subset $F$ of $\R$.
        \item $\{f > a\}$ is $\mu$-measurable for every $a\in\R$.
        \item $\{f <= a\}$ is $\mu$-measurable for every $a\in\R$.
        \item $\{f < a\}$ is $\mu$-measurable for every $a\in\R$.
        \item $\{f >= a\}$ is $\mu$-measurable for every $a\in\R$.
        \item $\{f \in I\}$ is $\mu$-measurable for every interval $I\subset\R$.
        \item $\{f \in I\}$ is $\mu$-measurable for every open interval $I\subset\R$.
        \item $\{f \in I\}$ is $\mu$-measurable for every closed interval $I\subset\R$.
    }
    \proof
    Since $\Mf_\mu$ is a $\sigma$-field, (iii) and (iv), (v) and (vi) are
    automatically equivalent, respectively. Now observe that
    $$
        \{f >= a\} = \bigcap_{n=1}^oo \{f > a - n^{-1}\},\quad
        \{f > a\} = \bigcup_{n=1}^oo \{f >= a + n^{-1}\}.
    $$
    As $\Mf_\mu$ is a $\sigma$-field, upper relation yields that (iii) and (vi)
    are equivalent. Hence, (iii)-(vi) are all equivalent.

    Notice that clearly all (iii)-(vi) imply (vii), and (vii) implies (viii) and
    (ix).
    In addition, since
    $$
        \{f > a\} = \bigcup_{n=1}^oo \{a < f < n\},\quad
        \{f >= a\} = \bigcup_{n=1}^oo \{a <= f <= n\},
    $$
    (viii) implies (iii), and (ix) implies (vi).

    Every open set in $\R$ can be expressed by a countable union of
    open intervals in $\R$. Thus (viii) implies (i). Also, clearly (i)
    implies (viii).
    Finally, observe that clearly (i) and (ii) are equivalent. This finishes
    the proof. \proved
}

Now we are going to study $\mu$-measurable $[-oo,oo]$-valued functions.
Such functions is needed if one consider supremum or infimum of functions.
Recall that the topology on $[-oo,oo]$ is given by an order topology where $-oo <= a$
and $oo >= a$ for every $a\in[-oo,oo]$.
Equivalently, following metric $d(x,y) = |\arctan x - \arctan y|$ which makes the space complete
and generates the same topology described in above.
Here, we define $\arctan oo \defeq \pi/2$, $\arctan (-oo) \defeq -\pi/2$.

useenv prop {
    Let $f:X->[-oo,oo]$ is $\mu$-measurable if and only if for every open subset $V$ of $\R$,
    $\{f\in V\}$, $\{f = oo\}$, and $\{f = -oo\}$ are $\mu$-measurable.
    \proof
    First, assume that $f$ is $\mu$-measurable, and fix any open subset $V$ of $\R$.
    Since both $[-oo,a)$ and $(a,oo]$ are open in $[-oo,oo]$ for every $a\in\R$,
    we have
    $$
        \{f = oo\} = \bigcap_{n=1}^oo \{f\in (n,oo]\} \in \Mf_\mu,\quad
        \{f = -oo\} = \bigcap_{n=1}^oo \{f\in [-oo,-n)\} \in \Mf_\mu.
    $$
    Notice that $V$ is also open in $[-oo,oo]$ because $\R$ is a subspace of $[-oo,oo]$.
    Hence, $\{f \in V\}$ is clearly $\mu$-measurable.

    Now assume the converse, and $W$ be any open subset of $[-oo,oo]$.
    The only cases we need to consider is that either $-oo\in W$ or $oo\in W$, without loss of
    generality, assume that $oo\in W$ and $-oo\notin W$.
    Since $W\cap\R$ is open in $\R$, by our assumption, $\{f\in W\cap\R\}$ is $\mu$-measurable.
    Therefore,
    $$
        \{f\in W\} = \{f\in W\cap\R\} \cup \{f = oo\} \in \Mf_\mu.
    $$
    This proves that $f$ is $\mu$-measurable. \proved
}

We already know that if $f$ and $g$ are real-valued continuous function on the
real line, then both $f+g$ and $fg$ are also continuous. Then one can question
about for measurability. Similar for continuity, if $f$ and $g$ are real-valued
$\mu$-measurable functions, then both $f+g$ and $fg$ are $\mu$-measurable.

useenv prop { \label{prop:mble-product-fnts-1}
    Let $f,g : X -> \R$ be $\mu$-measurable functions. Then $-f$, $f+g$ and $fg$
    are $\mu$-measurable.
    \proof
    The measurability of $-f$ is immediately follows from
    \Cref{prop:measurable-fnt}. Fix $a\in\R$. Then observe that
    $$
        \{f + g > a\} = \{f > a - g\}
        = \bigcup_{q\in\Q}\{f > q\}\cap\{g > a - q\}.
    $$
    Since $f$ and $g$ are $\mu$-measurable, and $\Mf_\mu$ is a $\sigma$-field,
    that identity implies that $\{f + g > a\}$ is $\mu$ measurable.
    As $a\in\R$ is arbitrary, we can conclude that $f+g$ is $\mu$-measurable.

    To prove that $fg$ is $\mu$-measurable, we use the following formula
    $$
        fg = {1//4}(f+g)^2 - {1//4}(f-g)^2.
    $$
    Hence, it suffices to show that $f^2$ is $\mu$-measurable.
    Fix $a\in\R$. If $a < 0$ then clearly $\{f^2 > a\} = X$, so we can further
    assume that $a>=0$. In this case,
    $$
        \{f^2 > a\}
        = \{|f| > \sqrt{a}\}
        = \{f >= 0, f > \sqrt{a}\} \cup \{f < 0, f < -\sqrt{a}\},
    $$
    and the right hand side set is indeed $\mu$-measurable by
    \Cref{prop:measurable-fnt}. \proved
}

useenv exec* { \label{defin:positive-negative-fnt}
    Let $f:X->\R$ be a function, and we define the positive part and the negative
    part of $f$ by\index[sym]{$f_+$}\index[sym]{$f_-$}
    $$
        f_+ \defeq f \vee 0,\quad f_- \defeq (-f)\vee 0.
    $$
    Prove that if $f$ is $\mu$-measurable, so are $f_+$ and $f_-$.

    In addition, if $g:X->\R$ is another $\mu$-measurable function, prove that
    both $f\vee g$ and $f\land g$ are $\mu$-measurable.
}

In the proof of \Cref{prop:mble-product-fnts-1}, we actually proved that $f^2$
and $|f|$ are $\mu$-measurable provided that $f$ is $\mu$-measurable.
One can notice that from \Cref{prop:measurable-fnt}, we can prove the following
result.

useenv prop {
    Let $Y$ and $Z$ are topological spaces, $f:X->Y$ be $\mu$-measurable
    function, and $\phi:Y->Z$ be a continuous function. Then $\phi\circ f$ is
    $\mu$-measurable.
    \proof
    Fix any open set $U$ in $Z$. Being continuous, $\phi^{-1}[U]$ is also open
    in $Y$. Therefore, since
    $$
        \{\phi\circ f \in U\} = \{f \in \phi^{-1}[U]\},
    $$
    we can conclude that $\phi\circ f$ is $\mu$-measurable. \proved
}

If $f$ is $\mu$-measurable, we can obtain very interesting result which used in the future.
#label(prop:measurable-and-semicontinuity)
useenv prop {
    Let $f:X -> \R$ be $\mu$-measurable function. Then the function
    $g(t) = \mu\{f > t\}$ is decreasing right continuous on $(0,oo)$.
    Furthermore, $g$ is LSC on $(0,oo)$.
    \proof
    For $0 < s < t < oo$, since $\{f > t\} \subset \{f > s\}$, we have
    $\mu\{f > t\}<=\mu\{f > s\}$. Thus the function $g$ is decreasing.
    To prove the right continuity, fix $t_0\in(0,oo)$ and sequence $(t_n)_n$ on $(0,oo)$
    such that $t_n \downarrow t_0$.
    Since $f$ is $\mu$-measurable, \Cref{thm:measure-continuity} and the identity
    $$
        \bigcup_{n=1}^oo \{ f > t_n \} = \{f > t\}
    $$
    implies $g(t_n) -> g(t_0)$ as $n->oo$. This proves the right continuity.

    To prove that $g$ is LSC, fix $t_0\in (0,oo)$ and real $y$ such that $y < g(t_0)$.
    Since $g$ is right continuous, there exists $\delta>0$ such that
    $g(t) > y$ holds for every $t\in [t_0,t_0+\delta)$.
    Since $g$ is decreasing, actually $g(t) > y$ holds for every $|t-t_0| < \delta$.
    Hence we proved that $g$ is LSC at $t_0$ since $y$ is arbitrary.
    Now recall that we take any point $t_0$ from $(0,oo)$, thus the proposition is proved. \proved
}

On the measure theory, although a proposition $\bf{P}(x)$ does not satisfies
for \it{all} $x\in X$, it might be a case that the collection of all points $x\in X$
at which the proposition $\bf{P}(x)$ is false has measure zero. In other words,
$$
    \mu\{x\in X : \bf{P}(x) #"is false"\} = 0.
$$
useenv defin [Almost Everywhere] {
    Let $(X,\mu)$ be a measure space and $\bf{P}(x)$ is a proposition with a
    parameter $x\in X$. We say that $\bf{P}(x)$ is true \it{almost everywhere}
    if
    $$
        \mu\{x\in X : \bf{P}(x) #"is false"\} = 0.
    $$
    We also use an abbriviation: $\bf{P}(x)$ is true ($\mu$-a.e.), or just
    $\bf{P}(x)$ (a.e.) if there is no confusion of the measure.
}

Let us consider two functions $f$ and $g$ on $X$. If we know that $f$ is $\mu$-measurable,
and $f = g$ (a.e.), what can we say about $g$ then?
Futhermore, if $(f_n)_n$ is a sequence of $\mu$-measurable functions and it converges to
$f$ (a.e.). Then what can we sat about $f$ then?
Below two proposition gives the answer. Especially, \Cref{prop:limit-measurability} is
originated by \cite{Dudley}.

#label(prop:measurable-almost-everywhere-equal)
useenv prop {
    Let $Y$ be a topological space, and
    $f$ and $g$ are functions from $X$ into $Y$ such that $f = g$ (a.e.).
    If $f$ is $\mu$-measurable, so is $g$.
    \proof
    Fix any open set $V$ in $Y$. Then we have
    $$
        \{g \in V\} = (\{f = g\} \cap \{f \in V\}) \cup \{f != g\}.
    $$
    Since $f = g$ (a.e.), $\mu\{f!=g\} = 0$, thus the set $\{f!=g\}$ is $\mu$-measurable
    by \Cref{exe:null-set-is-measurable}. Using \Cref{thm:caratheodory}, the set $\{f=g\}$
    is also $\mu$-measurable. Since we assumed that $f$ is $\mu$-measurable,
    the set $\{f\in V\}$ is also $\mu$-measurable because of the definition of the measurable
    functions.
    Combining these, we therefore conclude that $\{g\in V\}$ is $\mu$-measurable.
    Since $V$ can be any open sets in $Y$, we proved that $g$ is $\mu$-measurable. \proved
}

#label(prop:limit-measurability)
useenv prop {
    Let $Y$ be a perfectly normal space, and let $(f_n)_n$ be a sequence of
    $\mu$-measurable functions from $X$ into $Y$. If $f_n -> f$ (a.e.) for some $f : X -> Y$,
    then $f$ is also $\mu$-measurable.
    \proof
    Let $N$ be the collection of all $x\in X$ such that $f_n(x)$ converges to $f(x)$.
    Then $\mu(X-!N) = 0$ because $f_n->f$ almost everywhere.

    Fix any open set $V$ in $Y$. By \Cref{thm:vedenissof-thm}, there exists a continuous
    function $\phi:X->[0,1]$ such that $V = \{\phi > 0\}$.
    Define $F_n = \{\phi >= n^{-1}\}$. Clearly $F_n$ are closed because $\phi$ is continuous,
    and $\bigcup_n F_n = V$.

    Fix any $x\in \{f\in V\} \cap N$. Take a positive integer $k$ such that
    $f(x) \in \{\phi > k^{-1}\}$.
    Since $f_n(x) -> f(x)$ as $n->oo$, we can take sufficiently large $m$ so that
    $f_n(x)\in \{\phi > k^{-1}\}$ for every $n>=m$. From this fact, we obtain
    useenv align* {
        \{f\in V\} \cap N
        &= N\cap \bigcup_{k=1}^oo \{\phi\circ f > k^{-1}\} \\
        &\subset N\cap\bigcup_{k=1}^oo\bigcup_{m=1}^oo\bigcap_{n=m}^oo \{\phi\circ f_n > k^{-1}\} \\
        &\subset N\cap\bigcup_{k=1}^oo\bigcup_{m=1}^oo\bigcap_{n=m}^oo \{f_n\in F_k\} \\
        &\eqcolon N \cap E.
    }
    On the other hand, if $x\in N\cap E$, that means, there exist $k$ and $m$ such that
    $f_n(x)\in F_k$ for every $n>=m$ and $f_n(x) -> f(x)$ as $n->oo$.
    Since $F_k$ is closed, this implies $f(x)\in F_k$. Hence, we conclude that
    #eq (eq:limit-fnt-mble-1) {
        \{f\in V\}\cap N = N\cap\bigcup_{k=1}^oo\bigcup_{m=1}^oo\bigcap_{n=m}^oo \{f_n\in F_k\}.
    }

    As $X-!N$ is $\mu$-null set, it is $\mu$-measurable, so $N$ is also $\mu$-measurable.
    In addition, each $f_n$ are $\mu$-measurable, and $F_k$ are closed in $Y$.
    This implies that $\{f_n\in F_k\}$ is $\mu$-measurable for each $n$ and $k$.
    Therefore, \cref{eq:limit-fnt-mble-1} gives that $\{f\in V\}\cap N$ is $\mu$-measurable.
}

#label(remark:limit-measurability)
useenv remark {
    If one consider a measure space $(X,\Mf,\mu)$ described in \Cref{remark:def-other-textbooks},
    There is no guarantee that although $f = g$ (a.e.), $f$ is measurable implies
    $g$ is also measurable.
    This is because, unlike our setting, \lq\lq measurability\rq\rq\ is given from
    $\sigma$-field $\Mf$ not from a measure $\mu$. Which means, not
    only we cannot know the measurability of $\{f != g\}$, the formula
    $\mu\{f!=g\}=0$ is actually not defined because in general, the
    domain of the measure $\mu$ is $\Mf$.

    However, we started from a measure defined at \Cref{defin:measure} and define
    \lq\lq measurability\rq\rq\ using the meausre. Since the domain of
    our measure is the power set of the space, $\mu\{f!=g\}$ makes sense in the first place.
    Hence \Cref{prop:measurable-almost-everywhere-equal} makes sense also for our setting.
    This is the one of the benefit to define a measure like \Cref{defin:measure} not like
    in \Cref{remark:def-other-textbooks}.
}

useenv remark {
    \Cref{prop:limit-measurability} does not holds even when $Y$ is completely normal.
    Consider $Y = [0,1]\times[0,1]$ with lexicographic order.
    One can easily check that $Y$ is then a completely normal space (see
    \cite{counterexamples-topology}).
}

We have been studied the definition of $\mu$-measurable functions.
We end this section by showing one of the most important theorem about
$\mu$-measurable functions. Before that we need a definition.
useenv defin [Simple functions] { \label{defin:simple-functions}
    We say that a function is called \it{simple} if it takes only finitely many
    values.
}
